"""Explicit weather/climate authoring; supplied observations and calculations."""
from assemble_topic import assemble
FACTS={
'01':['Weather concerns atmospheric conditions at a place over a short period; climate concerns longer-term patterns and variability.','A short local record cannot establish a multi-decade regional trend merely by averaging its readings.'],
'02':['A Stevenson screen shields suitable sensors from direct radiation and precipitation while allowing airflow.','Temperature and humidity instruments need sheltered exposure, while rain, wind and sunshine instruments need different appropriate exposures.'],
'02-01':['White exterior, louvres, suitable height and representative siting reduce avoidable influences on air observations.','Door orientation should reduce direct sunlight during reading; a Northern Hemisphere instruction needs adjustment for the Southern Hemisphere.'],
'02-02':['Read and reset instruments consistently, avoid unnecessary warming/handling and record site or instrument problems.','A moved or damaged screen can change exposure and compromise direct comparison through time.'],
'03':['Weather instruments measure different variables with their own units, intervals and exposure needs.','Instrument identification should use working parts and purpose rather than position in a familiar diagram.'],
'03-01':['A rain gauge collects precipitation and uses a calibrated system to express equivalent rainfall depth.','A raw collecting-vessel volume is not directly the same number as rainfall depth without the collector calibration.'],
'03-02':['Maximum and minimum thermometers record extremes since reset, distinct from current temperature.','Reading index ends and labelled scales correctly is necessary, and an unreset interval needs documenting.'],
'03-03':['Wet-bulb evaporation cools the thermometer; paired dry/wet readings and a suitable table support humidity inference.','A dry wick or inadequate ventilation can invalidate an otherwise plausible wet/dry interpretation.'],
'03-04':['A barometer measures atmospheric pressure and a barograph records its changes through time.','Raw station pressure and pressure adjusted to a common reference should not be mixed uncritically.'],
'03-05':['An anemometer measures wind speed using a calibrated response, such as rotating cups.','A gust maximum and a period mean are different statistics and need comparable units and intervals.'],
'03-06':['A wind vane indicates source direction when correctly aligned and interpreted under its design convention.','A northerly wind comes from the north, and calm need not have a meaningful source direction.'],
'03-07':['A traditional sunshine recorder records qualifying bright-sunshine duration through traces on a time-marked card.','Unburnt gaps should not be counted as sunshine; sunshine duration differs from daylight.'],
'04':['Means, ranges and totals answer different statistical questions and must retain variable and interval.','Missing data, measured zero and compromised readings require distinct treatment.'],
'04-01':['Temperature range is highest minus lowest; a specified maximum/minimum midpoint is not automatically a continuous-record mean.','In the original 28°C maximum and 12°C minimum example, range is 16°C and midpoint is 20°C.'],
'04-02':['Comparable non-overlapping rainfall amounts can be summed into a period total.','For 0,4,6,2 mm, total is 12 mm, mean is 3 mm per day and range is 6 mm.'],
'04-03':['Relative humidity expresses moisture relative to saturation at the relevant temperature, not rainfall probability.','Dry 25°C and wet 20°C give 5°C depression; a supplied appropriate lookup of 63% is the relative humidity, not five percent.'],
'04-04':['Pressure is measured in hPa and its changes are not temperature changes.','Original values 1,016 to 1,008 hPa show an 8 hPa fall over the specified first-to-last interval.'],
'04-05':['Oktas express cloud amount in eighths, while cloud type concerns form/level and differs from coverage.','More cloud amount alone does not establish more rain at the ground.'],
'04-06':['Sunshine totals add recorded bright intervals and exclude gaps.','The original 2,2.5,1.5 hour intervals total six hours, distinct from daylight duration.'],
'04-07':['Wind speed needs a unit and averaging period; one m/s equals 3.6 km/h.','A sheltered local measurement can be valid for that microenvironment without representing an exposed regional station.'],
'04-08':['Wind direction is its source; a wind rose can summarise directional frequencies.','Directions near 350° and 10° cannot be averaged arithmetically to infer a southerly mean.'],
'05':['High and low pressure are relative pressure patterns, not temperature labels.','In the Southern Hemisphere surface low circulation is generally clockwise/inward and high circulation anticlockwise/outward.'],
'05-01':['Subsiding air is compressed and warms, commonly reducing relative humidity and deep-cloud formation under high pressure.','High pressure does not exclude every low cloud or fog situation.'],
'05-02':['Rising air expands and cools, with condensation possible when moisture and saturation conditions are suitable.','A low can be warm or dry; low pressure does not by itself establish coldness or measured rain.'],
'06':['Rainfall types are distinguished by lifting mechanism while sharing rising/cooling/condensation stages under suitable conditions.','Convectional, frontal/cyclonic and relief contributions can overlap in a real event.'],
'06-01':['Surface heating can generate buoyant ascent, expansion/cooling and cloud/precipitation when moisture and stability permit.','Heating alone does not ensure a shower in a dry or stable atmosphere.'],
'06-02':['Frontal lifting or convergence within a low can produce cyclonic rainfall under suitable moisture conditions.','A cold wedge lifting warm moist air differs from ascent over a mountain barrier.'],
'06-03':['Moist air forced over high ground can cool, condense and precipitate on the windward ascent.','Descending warming air can contribute to a leeward rain shadow, not a universal absence of rain.'],
'07':['Synoptic charts and satellite products have different variables, valid times and keys.','Isobar spacing supports a qualitative pressure-gradient inference, not an exact speed without further information.'],
'07-01':['Isobars join equal pressure at the stated reference, and adjacent labels determine the interval.','Original consecutive labels 1,012,1,008,1,004 hPa imply a 4 hPa interval and inward-decreasing pressure.'],
'07-02':['Visible and infrared satellite products represent different observations and depend on their stated keys.','Cloud imagery does not directly provide a station rainfall total or prove rain reaches the ground.'],
'08':['Latitude, altitude, currents, sea distance, prevailing winds and pressure systems interact to influence climate.','A climate graph describes a pattern but needs additional location/circulation evidence to attribute controls.'],
'08-01':['Solar angle and seasonal day length vary with latitude and contribute to broad temperature patterns.','Southern and Northern Hemisphere seasons differ in timing, so months must be interpreted with location.'],
'08-02':['Temperature generally decreases with altitude in the troposphere, with inversions and other exceptions.','Under a supplied 0.6°C/100 m model, an 800 m rise from 24°C gives an estimated 19.2°C.'],
'08-03':['Cold Benguela marine influence and upwelling contribute to Namibia’s cool coastal setting and fog conditions.','Near-surface fog and limited rainfall can coexist; a current alone does not explain every climate property.'],
'08-04':['Marine heat storage and redistribution commonly moderate coastal temperature ranges under suitable exposure.','The original coastal 22–16°C and inland 29–11°C monthly-mean extremes give ranges of 6°C and 18°C.'],
'08-05':['Prevailing wind influence depends on the air’s source route, land–sea orientation and modification.','A compass direction alone does not establish onshore flow without the coastline orientation.'],
'08-06':['Pressure-system patterns affect convergence, subsidence, ascent and seasonal moisture conditions.','Subsiding-air influence can suppress deep cloud under suitable conditions but is not interchangeable with temperature.'],
'91':['Weather investigation needs comparable observation schedules, exposures, units and traceable records.','A larger sample does not remove systematic exposure or timing differences unless the design addresses them.'],
'OBJ-6137-X.02':['Enhanced greenhouse forcing and ozone depletion involve different radiation mechanisms.','Long-term climate attribution requires appropriate records and evidence, not one isolated hot day.']}
CARDS={
'001':('Distinguish weather from climate.','Weather describes short-term atmospheric conditions at a place. Climate summarises longer-term patterns and variability, commonly from decades of observations. The difference concerns time scale and the evidence being summarised.',['1 mark for short-term weather; 1 for longer-term climate patterns; 1 for the explicit time/evidence difference.']),
'002':('Suggest which statement concerns weather and which climate: A gives today’s maximum as 29°C; B describes the seasonal pattern across a multi-decade record. Explain the evidence.','A concerns weather because it describes one day. B concerns climate because it summarises repeated seasonal observations over decades. Today’s value alone cannot replace that longer record.',['1 mark for applying weather to A; 1 for the given long-record reason for B; 1 for explaining the evidence-scale limitation.']),
'003':('Name the louvred shelter used to reduce direct radiation effects on suitable air-temperature instruments.','A Stevenson screen.',['1 mark for the name Stevenson screen.']),
'004':('Describe the design/exposure interpretation: A is a white louvred screen over grass; B is against a heated wall with blocked louvres and higher readings.','A permits ventilation while shielding sensors. B has plausible heat and airflow influences. The higher B values may reflect exposure differences, not proof of a regional climate contrast.',['1 mark for A’s shielding/airflow feature; 1 for B’s wall/ventilation evidence; 1 for the limited comparison interpretation.']),
'005':('Name the screen feature that allows air circulation while helping shield instruments.','Louvres or slatted sides.',['1 mark for the name louvres/slatted sides.']),
'006':('Name one record needed when a maximum–minimum thermometer was not reset at the usual time.','A note of the missed reset and the actual interval represented.',['1 mark for the name of a relevant reset/interval record.']),
'007':('Name the instrument used to measure atmospheric pressure.','A barometer.',['1 mark for the name barometer.']),
'008':('Describe the measured elements represented by the supplied original weather-instrument resource, using the working parts and units shown.','The funnel collector records rainfall depth, the paired wet/dry thermometers support relative-humidity inference, and rotating cups measure wind speed. Each identification should follow the actual depicted parts and key.',['1 mark for the collector/rainfall relationship; 1 for the paired-thermometer evidence; 1 for the cups/speed feature.']),
'009':('Describe the rain-gauge result: the supplied collector calibration is 20 mL per 1 mm rainfall and the measured volume is 120 mL over the stated day.','The calibrated rainfall depth is 6 mm. Volume is converted using this collector’s 20 mL/mm relationship. It is a daily result only because the collection interval is stated.',['1 mark for the calibrated depth feature; 1 for the 120/20 evidence; 1 for the interval/calibration interpretation without equating arbitrary volume and depth.']),
'010':('Describe the thermometer record: since the stated daily reset, the maximum index is 28°C, minimum index 12°C and current reading 20°C.','The recorded extreme range is 12–28°C. Current temperature is 20°C, distinct from either extreme. The extremes concern the reset interval rather than a forecast.',['1 mark for the min/max pattern; 1 for current-versus-extreme evidence; 1 for the reset-period interpretation.']),
'011':('Describe the hygrometer interpretation: dry bulb 25°C, functioning wet bulb 20°C; the supplied lookup for those conditions gives 63%.','Wet-bulb depression is 5°C. The lookup gives relative humidity of 63%, not 5%. Evaporative cooling of the functioning wet bulb explains the lower reading.',['1 mark for the 5°C difference feature; 1 for the lookup evidence; 1 for the evaporative-cooling relationship.']),
'012':('Describe the pressure trace: readings at 00:00,06:00,12:00 are 1,016,1,012,1,008 hPa.','Pressure falls steadily across the three observations, by 8 hPa from first to last. The full interval is twelve hours. The record is pressure, not an 8°C cooling.',['1 mark for the downward trend; 1 for the 8 hPa evidence; 1 for interpreting the time/variable correctly.']),
'013':('Describe the wind-speed comparison: A records a ten-minute mean of 20 km/h; B reports a gust of 30 km/h, with no B mea supplied.','The B gust value exceeds A’s mean numerically. The statistics differ, so B’s mean cannot be inferred. A like-for-like mean or gust comparison needs matching data.',['1 mark for the numerical contrast feature; 1 for the mean/gust evidence; 1 for the comparability limit.']),
'014':('Describe wind direction from this aligned-vane record: the instrument’s pointing end indicates the north marker under the stated source-direction convention.','The wind is northerly, coming from the north. It moves generally towards the south. Correct interpretation depends on the stated pointing-end convention and alignment.',['1 mark for the source-direction feature; 1 for the north-marker evidence; 1 for distinguishing origin from destination.']),
'015':('Describe the sunshine record: marked intervals are 08:00–10:00,11:00–13:30 and 15:00–16:30; gaps are unmarked.','Recorded sunshine totals six hours. The gaps are excluded. The result is bright-sunshine duration, not the entire 8.5-hour first-to-last span or the day’s daylight length.',['1 mark for the discontinuous-interval pattern; 1 for the six-hour evidence; 1 for interpreting duration rather than including gaps/daylight.']),
'016':('Calculate the four-day rainfall total and mean daily rainfall from 0,4,6,2 mm.','Total is 12 mm; mean is 12/4 = 3 mm per day.',['1 mark for the sum method; 1 for the correct total and division by four; 1 for the mean result with the appropriate units/interval.']),
'017':('Describe the main patterns in the supplied supplied resource: three daily maxima 24,28,26°C; rainfall 0,8,4 mm; sunshine 8,3,6 hours. Keep its axes separate.','Temperature peaks on day 2 at 28°C before falling to 26°C. Rainfall also peaks on day 2 at 8 mm. Sunshine is lowest that day at 3 hours, compared with 8 on day 1 and 6 on day 3.',['1 mark for a correct temperature pattern with resource evidence; 1 for a correctly supported rainfall feature; 1 for sunshine interpretation using its own unit and interval.']),
'018':('Describe the temperature statistics: maximum 28°C and minimum 12°C; the task defines mean estimate as their midpoint.','Range is 16°C. The specified midpoint estimate is 20°C. That estimate is not automatically the continuous-record daily mean.',['1 mark for the range feature; 1 for the midpoint calculation evidence; 1 for the method limitation.']),
'019':('Describe the rainfall statistics for the original four-day set 0,4,6,2 mm.','Total is 12 mm, mean daily amount 3 mm and daily range 6 mm. They represent accumulation, average and spread respectively.',['1 mark for the total feature; 1 for mean using the data; 1 for range and the distinction of operations.']),
'020':('Describe what these humidity observations support: dry/wet readings coincide with a wet wick and suitable ventilation, while a second equal-reading record has a dry wick.','The functioning pair supports saturation and 100% relative humidity. The dry-wick record is compromised. Equality there is not reliable evidence of the same atmospheric state.',['1 mark for the functioning-pair saturation feature; 1 for the wick-condition evidence; 1 for the restricted second-record interpretation.']),
'021':('Describe the variable distinction: pressure falls from 1,016 to 1,008 hPa while recorded air temperature rises from 20 to 24°C.','Pressure decreases by 8 hPa while temperature increases by 4°C. The opposite changes show that low/falling pressure cannot simply be read as low/falling temperature.',['1 mark for the pressure trend with units; 1 for the temperature evidence; 1 for the distinction between variables.']),
'022':('Describe the cloud contrast: A has 2 oktas of high thin cloud; B has 6 oktas of low layered cloud; neither has a rainfall reading.','B has more sky covered and a different cloud type. The observations do not directly establish a larger rain total at B. Amount and type are distinct from measured precipitation.',['1 mark for coverage pattern; 1 for type/okta evidence; 1 for the limit on rainfall inference.']),
'023':('Describe the sunshine statistics: three comparable daily totals are 4,6,8 hours, with the other four days unobserved.','Observed total is 18 hours and mean for recorded days is six hours. The missing days cannot be treated as zero to create a complete-week mean.',['1 mark for the recorded-day pattern; 1 for the 18/3 evidence; 1 for correct missing-data interpretation.']),
'024':('Describe the speed record: comparable readings are 2,4,6 m/s at a sheltered local site.','Mean and range are both numerically 4 m/s but represent different summaries. The record concerns the sheltered site and sampled interval, not automatically an exposed regional station.',['1 mark for the numerical summary feature; 1 for the 2/4/6 evidence; 1 for the site/interval interpretation.']),
'025':('Describe the wind-frequency record: N2,E6,S1,W1 plus two calm intervals.','East is the most frequent source direction. Its six records are 60% of ten directional observations or 50% of all twelve intervals. Calm must remain a separate category, so the denominator needs stating.',['1 mark for the prevailing-direction pattern; 1 for the count/denominator evidence; 1 for correct calm treatment.']),
'026':('Explain why sinking air in a high often discourages deep cloud, while rising moist air in a low can favour it.','Descending air is compressed and warms, which can reduce relative humidity. Rising air expands and cools; with enough moisture it can reach saturation and condense. The result depends on conditions, not pressure being a temperature label.',['1 mark for descent/compression as a cause; 1 for the warming–humidity link; 1 for ascent/expansion/cooling; 1 for the conditional condensation effect.']),
'027':('Describe the contrast in this Southern Hemisphere resource: A has a relative high and descending warming air; B a relative low with converging moist air rising and cooling.','A supports subsidence and a tendency towards reduced deep-cloud formation. B supports ascent and possible condensation under its moist conditions. The pressure labels do not themselves state the temperatures.',['1 mark for the A vertical-motion feature; 1 for B’s moisture/ascent evidence; 1 for the conditional comparison.']),
'028':('Describe the high-pressure interpretation: a Southern Hemisphere chart shows a relative pressure maximum, generally outward anticlockwise surface flow and descending air.','The centre is a high. The circulation and descent match the supplied hemisphere. Descent commonly warms the air and can reduce relative humidity, without excluding all cloud or fog.',['1 mark for the relative-maximum feature; 1 for the circulation/descent evidence; 1 for a conditional weather interpretation.']),
'029':('Describe the low-pressure interpretation: a Southern Hemisphere chart has inward-decreasing pressure and clockwise converging flow, with moist air rising.','The centre is a low. Convergence and ascent are consistent with it. Rising moist air can cool to saturation, supporting cloud development if suitable conditions are met.',['1 mark for the pressure pattern; 1 for the flow/ascent evidence; 1 for conditional cloud interpretation.']),
'030':('Describe the common cloud-forming sequence after moist air is lifted.','The air rises into lower surrounding pressure and expands. It cools and can reach saturation. Condensation forms cloud, with precipitation possible after further suitable development.',['1 mark for the first ascent/expansion step; 1 for cooling/saturation; 1 for cloud and conditional precipitation as the result.']),
'031':('Describe how the lifting mechanisms differ in these resources: A heated surface, B a mountain facing moist flow, C cold air advancing beneath warm moist air.','A supports convective lifting. B supports relief/orographic ascent. C supports frontal/cyclonic lifting. Cooling can follow ascent in each, but the triggers differ.',['1 mark for A’s heating relationship; 1 for B’s barrier evidence; 1 for C’s air-mass lifting feature.']),
'032':('Describe convectional evidence: morning surface heating is followed by buoyant ascent, cloud growth and an afternoon shower, with moisture recorded.','The sequence links heating to rising air. Ascent permits expansion and cooling. Recorded moisture and cloud/shower development support convectional rainfall rather than heating alone proving rain.',['1 mark for the heating/ascent pattern; 1 for cooling evidence/sequence; 1 for the conditional moisture-to-rain interpretation.']),
'033':('Describe frontal-rainfall evidence: a wedge of advancing cold dense air lifts warm moist air along a boundary where cloud and rain are recorded.','The cold wedge forces warm air upward. Rising air can expand and cool. Cloud/rain along the boundary support frontal cyclonic rainfall, not ascent over a mountain.',['1 mark for the air-mass contact feature; 1 for the lifting/cooling evidence; 1 for the frontal interpretation.']),
'034':('Describe the relief-rainfall pattern: moist air approaches a ridge from the west, rises with cloud/rain on that side and descends warming to the east.','The west side is windward in this record. Uplift and cooling support cloud/rain there. The eastern descent can contribute to relatively drier leeward conditions, not guaranteed absence of rain.',['1 mark for the windward location feature; 1 for the ascent/rain evidence; 1 for the conditional lee contrast.']),
'035':('Calculate the isobar interval from consecutive labels 1,012,1,008,1,004 hPa.','Each adjacent difference is 4 hPa.',['1 mark for the adjacent-label subtraction method; 1 for the correct 4 result; 1 for the pressure unit hPa.']),
'036':('Describe the pressure/cloud relationship in the supplied original Southern Hemisphere synoptic chart and keyed satellite resource: centre 1,004 hPa, outer isobar 1,012 hPa, and a keyed cloud band aligned with an eastern front at the same 12:00 valid time.','Pressure decreases inward from 1,012 to 1,004 hPa, identifying a low. The cloud band aligns with the eastern front at the same valid time, supporting frontal cloud. It does not establish a gauge rainfall total.',['1 mark for the pressure pattern using labels; 1 for cloud/front positional evidence; 1 for a qualified rainfall interpretation.']),
'037':('Describe the isobar pattern on the supplied synoptic chart: values decrease inward 1,012→1,008→1,004 hPa and the lines are closer together on the western side at the same map scale.','The centre is relatively low pressure. The interval is 4 hPa. Closer western spacing supports a stronger pressure-gradient interpretation there, not an exact speed value.',['1 mark for the low-centre pattern; 1 for the interval evidence; 1 for the qualified spacing interpretation.']),
'038':('Describe the satellite inference: a keyed infrared product shows a cold cloud-top band near a mapped front, but no station rain observations are supplied.','The band identifies cold cloud tops under the product key. Its alignment is consistent with frontal cloud. It does not directly measure rainfall reaching a particular gauge.',['1 mark for the keyed cloud feature; 1 for alignment evidence; 1 for the ground-rain limitation.']),
'039':('Explain how two supplied controls can contribute to a cool dry coastal setting: cold marine water and frequent subsiding-air influence.','Cold water cools near-surface marine air and can support fog under suitable moisture conditions. Subsidence can warm air aloft and inhibit deep cloud development. Together they can support cool foggy but low-rainfall conditions; latitude alone does not determine the result.',['1 mark for cold water as a factor; 1 for its near-surface cooling/moisture link; 1 for subsidence and limited deep ascent; 1 for the combined conditional climate effect.']),
'040':('Describe the controls in the supplied supplied resource: A and B share 23°S latitude; A is a low coastal site beside cold water with subsiding air, while B is 1,000 m high with moist air rising over relief.','Shared latitude alone cannot explain all differences. A’s cold coastal and subsiding-air setting can support cool surface conditions with fog but limited deep rainfall. At B, height can lower temperature relative to a comparable lower reference and moist uplift can favour rain.',['1 mark for a correctly described location/control feature; 1 for using the actual map evidence; 1 for a connected conditional climate interpretation.']),
'041':('Name the positional factor that changes solar angle and broad seasonal day-length patterns from Equator towards poles.','Latitude.',['1 mark for the name latitude.']),
'042':('Name the height-related control used in a supplied temperature-lapse-rate calculation.','Altitude or elevation.',['1 mark for the name altitude/elevation.']),
'043':('Name the cold marine-current system associated with Namibia’s coastal climate.','The Benguela system/current.',['1 mark for the name Benguela.']),
'044':('Name the climate control illustrated by smaller temperature ranges at a suitably comparable coastal site than inland.','Distance from the sea or marine influence.',['1 mark for the name of the control: distance from sea/marine influence.']),
'045':('Name the wind statistic describing the source direction occurring most frequently during the relevant period.','Prevailing wind direction.',['1 mark for the name prevailing wind direction.']),
'046':('Name the large-scale atmospheric control involving high/low patterns and their seasonal shifts.','Pressure systems or atmospheric circulation.',['1 mark for the name pressure systems/atmospheric circulation.']),
'047':('Describe a repeatable school weather-recording procedure for comparing two sites.','Define the variables, times and units. Use suitable comparable exposures and simultaneous or appropriately matched readings. Record values, reset/collection intervals and any missing or compromised observations before calculating summaries.',['1 mark for the first defined-variable/time step; 1 for comparable measurement procedure; 1 for the traceable record as the result.']),
'048':('Explain why a noon-versus-dawn temperature comparison cannot attribute the entire difference to site location.','Time of day affects temperature as well as location. Unmatched times confound the comparison, so simultaneous or time-controlled observations are needed to isolate a site effect more convincingly.',['1 mark for the time-of-day reason; 1 for the consequence for causal interpretation and a targeted improvement.']),
'049':('Explain why enhanced greenhouse warming and stratospheric ozone depletion are different mechanisms.','Greenhouse gases interact with outgoing infrared radiation and affect energy balance. Stratospheric ozone absorbs much harmful ultraviolet radiation. Treating warming as simply sunlight entering an ozone hole confuses different radiation processes.',['1 mark for the greenhouse cause/factor; 1 for the infrared link and warming influence; 1 for ozone’s ultraviolet role; 1 for the effect of distinguishing the mechanisms rather than conflating them.']),
'050':('Describe the evidence-scale contrast: A is one hot afternoon’s maximum, B a comparable multi-decade temperature series showing a trend.','A describes weather at a short time scale. B is more appropriate evidence of longer-term change. Attributing the trend uniquely still needs broader mechanism and source-quality evidence.',['1 mark for the short-versus-long pattern; 1 for the time-series evidence distinction; 1 for the attribution limit.']),
'051':('Explain the error in treating rainfall total, mean and range as interchangeable. Use 0,4,6,2 mm as the test.','The confusion is between accumulation, average and spread. The total is 12 mm, mean 3 mm per day and range 6 mm. Different operations on the same set discriminate the three measures.',['1 mark for the statistical-operation confusion; 1 for the correct distinction applied through a test using the three results.']),
'052':('Explain the error in saying “low pressure means low temperature, and sinking air necessarily makes rain”. Use air motion and moisture as the test.','Pressure and temperature are different variables. Sinking air generally compresses and warms, often reducing relative humidity; rising moist air can cool and condense. The test follows motion, temperature change and moisture rather than the word low.',['1 mark for the pressure/temperature and vertical-motion confusion; 1 for the correct conditional ascent/descent distinction applied as a test.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['02','03','04'],'prompt':'(a) Name the instrument that records atmospheric pressure. [1]\n(b) Describe three design/siting features of a Stevenson screen and their purposes. [3]\n(c) Name the instrument used for wind speed. [1]\n(d) Describe the total-versus-mean rainfall distinction using four daily values 0,4,6,2 mm. [2]','answer':'(a) Barometer. (b) White exterior reduces radiation heating; louvres allow ventilation; suitable raised representative siting reduces direct ground/local-surface influence. (c) Anemometer. (d) Total accumulation is 12 mm, while mean is 3 mm per day; they summarise different quantities.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for barometer.','(b) 3 A marks for three relevant feature–purpose points; do not double credit the same shielding point.','(c) 1 A mark for anemometer.','(d) 2 B marks for interpreting the given total and mean with units/interval.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['04','05','07'],'prompt':'An original three-day station record gives daily maxima 24,28,26°C; rainfall 0,8,4 mm; sunshine 8,3,6 hours. Pressure falls from 1,016 to 1,008 hPa across the same stated period.\n(a) Name the rainfall-collecting instrument. [1]\n(b) Describe three supported patterns across the variables. [3]\n(c) Calculate the total rainfall and give its unit. [2]\n(d) What judgement is supported about a claim that falling pressure must mean falling temperature throughout this record? [1]','answer':'(a) Rain gauge. (b) Maximum temperature peaks at 28°C on day 2 then falls; rain peaks at 8 mm on day 2; sunshine is lowest at 3 hours on that day and rises later. (c) 0+8+4 = 12 mm. (d) The claim is not supported: temperature initially rises while pressure is recorded falling overall, and the variables are not interchangeable; exact pressure timing would be needed for every step.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for rain gauge.','(b) 3 B marks for three distinct data-supported patterns, with values or clear temporal evidence.','(c) 2 B marks for the correct addition/result and mm unit.','(d) 1 C mark for a supported judgement that the claim is not established by these different variables and timing coverage; an unsupported yes/no is insufficient.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'B'),('c','Calculate',2,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['01','02','91'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that this one-day school comparison proves a regional climate difference? Site A is read at dawn in a ventilated screen over grass; site B is read at noon beside a sun-heated wall with obstructed louvres. B is warmer. Judge whether time, exposure and record length allow that conclusion. [5]','answer':'Disagree. Different reading times and exposure offer plausible explanations for the warmer B value. The one-day record concerns weather and cannot establish a regional long-term climate contrast. Comparable times, suitable documented exposure and a much longer representative record are needed for the claimed conclusion. The observed difference can remain valid for those particular conditions without proving the broader claim.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judgement of timing, exposure, one-day versus climate scale and a targeted improvement linked to the stated weaknesses. Use the given evidence rather than generic more-data advice.','1 C mark for a supported overall decision distinguishing the local observation from the unsupported regional-climate conclusion.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]}
]
SOURCES=[('METOFFICE-INSTRUMENTS','https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/binaries/content/assets/metofficegovuk/pdf/weather/guides/10_0230_fs_17_observations.pdf'),('METOFFICE-EXPOSURE','https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/how-forecasts-are-made/observations/obs-critical-for-weather--climate'),('METOFFICE-HUMIDITY','https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/types-of-weather/humidity'),('METOFFICE-SUNSHINE','https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/guides/observations/how-we-measure-sunshine'),('NOAA-SOUTHERN-PRESSURE','https://ocean.weather.gov/product_description/keyterm.php'),('METOFFICE-WEATHER','https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/met-office-for-schools/other-content/other-resources/understanding-weather'),('METOFFICE-CLIMATE','https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/api/assets/file/factsheet_16-world-climates_2023pdf?prefix=assets'),('NASA-BENGUELA','https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/namibia-3464/'),('NOAA-OZONE','https://csl.noaa.gov/assessments/ozone/2022/twentyquestions/')]
if __name__=='__main__':assemble('1.6',FACTS,CARDS,TASKS,SOURCES)
